WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.300
<v Lecturer>If you wanted to know</v>

2
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.500
how events in AWS affects your services and your resources,

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.210
then you would use the personal health dashboard.

4
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.830
So, this is the little bell icon you see

5
00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.890
on the top right corner of your console,

6
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.560
and it is a global service

7
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.400
and it will show how outages happening within AWS

8
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.150
will directly impact you.

9
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.590
It will show you the impact on your resources

10
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.870
so you can see if your EC2 instances or EBS volumes

11
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.500
have maintenance upcoming,

12
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.420
and it will list issues

13
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.520
and actions you can do to remediate them.

14
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.860
So, the important thing to know is that it show you all

15
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.220
the maintenance events from AWS

16
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.830
and this is a keyword to look for in the exam.

17
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.210
You can also access all this information programmatically

18
00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.480
using an API called the AWS Health API.

19
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.620
And finally, there's a capability,

20
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.010
if you have enabled AWS Organizations,

21
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.640
to aggregate all the information

22
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.040 line:15% 
from all your accounts in your organization in one place,

23
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.960 line:15% 
and here is the URL in case you need it.

24
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.190
Now, let's talk about health event notifications.

25
00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.020
So, you can use if EventBridge or CloudWatch Events to react

26
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.654
to changes to your health events for your accounts.

27
00:01:05.654 --> 00:01:09.320
So we have the personal health dashboard that will trigger

28
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.410
an event in CloudWatch Events or EventBridge, okay?

29
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:13.243
For example, you may want

30
00:01:13.243 --> 00:01:15.970
to receive notification whenever EC2 instances

31
00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.450
in your accounts are scheduled for updates.

32
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.650
In this case, you would set up this notification

33
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.490
and then you would line it up with a target.

34
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.470
So this can not be used to return public events,

35
00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.610
so, all the service health dashboard events

36
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:28.880
cannot be intercepted here.

37
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.770
They have to be using the RSS if you remember.

38
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.090
But with this, we can enable use cases

39
00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.910
to capture notifications for our accounts.

40
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.340
For example, sending notifications,

41
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.410
capture event information,

42
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.160
or take automatic corrective actions,

43
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.260
if you know how to do so using some automation.

44
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.460
So, CloudWatch Events or EventBridge can invoke Lambda,

45
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.400
SNS, SQS, or Kinesis Data Streams.

46
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.840
And then for example, if you have Lambda,

47
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.630
you can do whatever you want with it

48
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.880
and take some corrective action

49
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.650
if this is something that happens a lot in your accounts.

50
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.730
So, that's it.

51
00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.203
Now let's have a look at the health dashboard.

52
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.850
So what I'm going to do now is click on this bell icon.

53
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.810
As you can see right now, there's zero open issues,

54
00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.240
schedule changes, or other notifications.

55
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.520
But I can click on "Event Log"

56
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.220
and see what has happened in my accounts.

57
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:15.350
And so, as you can see, some days, for example, June 30th,

58
00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.050
there was an Athena engine version auto upgrade notification

59
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.220
and one entity was affected

60
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.970
or an EC2 instance recovery, no action,

61
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.780
because it's something I triggered during my recording

62
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.270
and it affected one resource.

63
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.040
So you can see all these kind of issues

64
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.270
and whether or not they were closed.

65
00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.680
For example, there was an EC2 operational issue right here,

66
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.690
and it gives you the region, so US-East-2,

67
00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:38.523
the category,

68
00:02:38.523 --> 00:02:40.760
whether or not it affected some resources,

69
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.640
and what was the description of that issue,

70
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.060
which was right here.

71
00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.380
You can also get some information

72
00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.410
around the affected resources here if you have many.

73
00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.390
So this becomes very, very, very helpful.

74
00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.910
Then there is a dashboard, okay?

75
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.380
And the dashboard shows you how many issues

76
00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.790
in the past seven days, scheduled changes,

77
00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.710
and other notifications.

78
00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:01.810
And as you can see,

79
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.000
you can create a CloudWatch Event rule

80
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.929
to create rules and receive notifications for events

81
00:03:05.929 --> 00:03:08.880
that might affect your infrastructure.

82
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.740
So let's have a look at it.

83
00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.190
So what I'm going to do is go to CloudWatch Events,

84
00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.190
but actually I'm going to go into EventBridge

85
00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.593
to show you the newer interface, because they're similar.

86
00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.650
And here we have a rule, and we can create a rule,

87
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.137
and I'll call it "PHD Demo Rule."

88
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.440
And then we have to look for an event pattern by service

89
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.730
and the service provider is going to be "AWS"

90
00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.090
and the service name is going to be "Health," okay?

91
00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.410
And then we select an event type.

92
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.010
So we can have all events or specific health events,

93
00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.370
but we're going to use "All Events."

94
00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.700
And then it shows you what a simple event can be for this.

95
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.440
So for example, this is an elastic load balancing issue

96
00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.620
with a start date and an end date and so on.

97
00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.130
So we can get notified of all these events right here

98
00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.670
through CloudWatch Events.

99
00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:04.460
And then, the cool thing is that you can select targets

100
00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:05.860
and invoke a Lambda function

101
00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.290
or send a message to an SNS topic and so on,

102
00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.960
or all really the destinations are listed right here

103
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.890
for your health events to be intercepted.

104
00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.030
So this is one way of doing it.

105
00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.248
And the other way is to query all this information

106
00:04:17.248 --> 00:04:19.910
using the health API.

107
00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:24.000
As you can see, you can also get an organization-wide view.

108
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.840
And so the idea is that you set up organizations

109
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.320
and then you enable organizational view for AWS Health,

110
00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.070
and you'll be good to go.

111
00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.078
And I'm pretty lucky because I just got an issue

112
00:04:33.078 --> 00:04:34.460
with my PHD.

113
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.930
So I'm trying to launch to EC2 instances right now

114
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.500
for another lecture.

115
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.160
And it turns out that they're

116
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.370
in pending states for a long time.

117
00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.310
And for me this looks a bit odd.

118
00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.930
So, I looked at my personal health dashboards

119
00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.160
and see that there's one open issue.

120
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.846
So I can click on it and see that

121
00:04:51.846 --> 00:04:54.020
there is an open issue that's affecting me right now.

122
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:54.980
So I'm pretty unlucky,

123
00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.140
but pretty lucky regarding recording purpose.

124
00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.020
And so, as we can see, in EU-Central-1

125
00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.680
there is an EC2 operational issue.

126
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.651
And the event data is showing me that

127
00:05:04.651 --> 00:05:07.660
there is an instance connectivity issue.

128
00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.120
So there is increased error rates

129
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.870
and latencies for the EC2 API

130
00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.000
and connectivity issues for some instances

131
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.120
within a single AZ in the EU central region.

132
00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.310
So it doesn't detect that my instances are affected.

133
00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.230
It's probably because my instances are

134
00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.920
not launched just yet, but as you can see,

135
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.707
this was shown in my PHD.

136
00:05:25.707 --> 00:05:29.631
And so I can explain what is happening regarding my

137
00:05:29.631 --> 00:05:31.150
EC2 instances just yet,

138
00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.240
even though I find it extremely annoying.

139
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.470
So, the only thing I have to do is to wait

140
00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.670
for this to be resolved.

141
00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.770
So hopefully that makes sense of what you understand now,

142
00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.130
the usefulness of the service.

143
00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.110
I hope you liked this lecture

144
00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.060
and I will see you in the next lecture.

